{"name":"Marmalade Glaze","hex":"#c27545","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"marmalade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of jam made with citrus fruit, distinguished by being made slightly bitter by the addition of the peel and by partial caramelisation during manufacture. Most commonly made with Seville oranges, and usually qualified by the name of the fruit when made with other types of fruit. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Quince jam."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spread marmalade on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/marmalade"},{"term":"glaze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The vitreous coating of pottery or porcelain; anything used as a coating or color in glazing.","A transparent or semi-transparent layer of paint."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To install windows.","To apply a thin, transparent layer of coating."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glaze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Marmalade","blocks":[{"text":"Marmalade is a sweet, tangy fruit preserve made from the juice and peel of citrus fruits boiled with sugar and water. The well-known version is made from bitter orange, but other citrus fruits such as lemons and limes can also be used. Bitter orange is mostly used in marmalade because of its high pectin content, which gives a thick consistency to its marmalade. In addition, the balance of acid and pectin is needed for consistency. Fruits with low pectin have it added to make the marmalade."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg/330px-Homemade_marmalade%2C_England.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marmalade","wikidata":"Q5834964"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Mary_Kettilby%27s_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_%28year_1714%29_%28page_78-79%29.png/500px-Mary_Kettilby%27s_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_%28year_1714%29_%28page_78-79%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Mary_Kettilby%27s_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_%28year_1714%29_%28page_78-79%29.png/500px-Mary_Kettilby%27s_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_%28year_1714%29_%28page_78-79%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mary Kettilby's 1714 book A Collection of Above Three Hundred Receipts, pages 78–79","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMary_Kettilby's_A_Collection_of_Above_Three_Hundred_Receipts_book_(year_1714)_(page_78-79).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg/500px-FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg/1280px-FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg/500px-FollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Antique marmalade cutter, used to cut citrus fruit peel into thin slices","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFollowsAndBateMarmaladeCutter.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Marmalade_spread_on_bread.jpg/500px-Marmalade_spread_on_bread.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Marmalade_spread_on_bread.jpg/1280px-Marmalade_spread_on_bread.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wiki